  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Delaware saw $205M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 3,149 awards and an average award size of $65.2K. Obligations fell 44.2% year over year. Fiscal-year comparisons show FY21 at $321M, FY22 at $81.3M, FY23 at $325M, FY24 at $337M, and FY25 at $368M. The Department of Defense was the largest buyer with $92.5M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $42.8M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $18.3M. STONEWIN INTERNATIONAL, LLC was the top vendor at $16.7M, with CAESAR RODNEY SCHOOL DISTRICT at $15M and AQUIA NAVA II LLC at $14.6M. Other Computer Related Services led industries at $24.2M, followed by Petroleum Refineries at $16.7M and Elementary and Secondary Schools at $15M. This snapshot covers partial fiscal-year totals. Use the listed agency, vendor, and industry figures when comparing procurement activity or prioritizing outreach in Delaware for the remainder of FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are driving federal spending in Delaware?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $92.5M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $42.8M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $18.3M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Justice at $10.9M and the Department of Homeland Security at $9.6M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top vendors and which industries get the most awards?",
        "body": "STONEWIN INTERNATIONAL, LLC received $16.7M and CAESAR RODNEY SCHOOL DISTRICT received $15M in FY2026 to date. Other top vendors include AQUIA NAVA II LLC at $14.6M and CORBALT, LLC at $10.8M. Other Computer Related Services led industries at $24.2M, followed by Petroleum Refineries at $16.7M and Elementary and Secondary Schools at $15M."
      }
    ]
